  "markdown": "## Business Model\n\nCostco operates membership warehouses and e-commerce sites based on the concept of offering low prices on a limited selection of nationally-branded and private-label products in a wide range of categories to produce high sales volumes and rapid inventory turnover [10-K line 00134]. The company buys most merchandise directly from suppliers, routing it through cross-docking consolidation points or directly to warehouses. This approach creates freight volume and handling efficiencies that lower distribution costs compared to traditional multiple-step channels [10-K line 00135].\n\nCostco's warehouses are designed for efficiency in selling space, merchandise handling, and inventory control, with floor plans not emphasizing elaborate decoration. The company strictly controls entrances and exits and uses a membership format to reduce inventory losses (shrinkage) below those of typical retail operations [10-K line 00139].\n\nThe business model emphasizes high sales volumes and rapid inventory turnover achieved through volume purchasing, efficient distribution, and reduced handling in no-frills, self-service warehouse facilities. These factors enable Costco to operate profitably at significantly lower gross margins than most other retailers [10-K line 00134].\n\n## Competitive Position\n\nCostco competes globally with a wide array of wholesalers and retailers, including supermarkets, supercenters, online retailers, gasoline stations, hard discounters, department and specialty stores, and operators selling single categories or narrow ranges of merchandise [10-K line 00190]. In the U.S., significant competitors include Walmart, Target, Kroger, and Amazon, as well as other warehouse clubs such as Sam’s Club and BJ’s Wholesale Club [10-K line 00190].\n\nThe company differentiates itself through its membership format, which reinforces member loyalty and provides continuing fee revenue. It also leverages private-label brands like Kirkland Signature that are perceived by members as high quality and offered at prices generally lower than national brands [10-K line 00192].\n\n## Catalysts\n\nKey catalysts for Costco include:\n\n- Membership growth: Costco’s membership base has grown steadily, reaching 84,100 thousand paid members in 2026 [10-K line 00161].\n- Executive membership penetration: Executive members represented 42,300 thousand of total paid members in 2026, or 50.3% of the total [10-K line 00166].\n- Comparable sales growth: The company achieved comparable sales growth of 8% in 2026, driven by increases in average ticket and shopping frequency [10-K line 00451].\n- E-commerce expansion: Digitally-enabled comparable sales increased 21% during 2026 [10-K line 00452].\n- Opening new warehouses: Costco opened 28 new warehouses in 2026, including three relocations, for a total of 25 net new warehouses [10-K line 00415].\n\n## Recent Developments\n\nIn 2026, Costco reported the following significant developments:\n\n- Net sales increased by 10% to $297,247 million [10-K line 00433].\n- Membership fee revenue rose 11% to $5,907 million [10-K line 00458].\n- Net income grew 14% to $9,226 million or $20.76 per diluted share [10-K line 00422].\n- The Board of Directors approved a 13% increase in the quarterly cash dividend [10-K line 00423].\n- IEEPA tariff refunds were received, with $184 million recognized as a reduction to merchandise costs and $10 million recorded as interest income [10-K line 00426].\n- The company opened 28 new warehouses in 2026, including three relocations, for a total of 25 net new warehouses [10-K line 00415].\n\nCostco reported that its membership renewal rate was 92.3% in the U.S. and Canada, and 89.8% worldwide at the end of 2026 [10-K line 00167]. The company also noted that its average warehouse space is approximately 148,000 square feet [10-K line 00139].\n\nIn the fourth quarter of fiscal year 2026, net income was $2.998 billion or $6.75 per diluted share, up from $2.610 billion or $5.87 per diluted share in the same period last year [release line 00020].\n\nThe company operates 939 warehouses globally as of August 30, 2026 [release line 00021], with e-commerce sites operating in the U.S., Canada, the U.K., Mexico, Korea, Taiwan, Japan, Australia, and China [10-K line 00130].",
